/P 162 0 R
/E (Layered defenses significantly reduce vulnerability, but baseline models remain highly susceptible to context-building injections.)
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
351 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 162 0 R
/E (Baseline Vulnerability: Most models exhibit an ASR > 80% without defenses, confirming the high risk of multi-turn attacks.)
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
352 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 162 0 R
/E (M2 Dominance: The I/O Gate \(M2\) provides the steepest reduction in ASR \(dropping to ~20% in some cases\).)
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
353 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 162 0 R
/E (Model Variance: Models like GPT-OSS 120B respond better to Prompt Hardening \(M1\) than smaller models like Gemma.)
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
354 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 162 0 R
/E (The Usability Threshold: Most models under Baseline/M1 maintain an ORR below 15%, which is acceptable for production.)
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
355 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 162 0 R
/E (Gemma and GPT-OSS see ORR spikes to 94% and 88% respectively under I/O Gating. These models are essentially unusable under M2; they block almost 9 out of 10 benign user requests.)
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
356 0 obj
<<
